      "headline": "Making the world just that little bit better! | Alt-MBA | Cambridge GMCA | Applied Ethics + AI + Tech Leadership",
      "about": "Curiosity-driven technologist, social entrepreneur, and explorer with a passion for tech for good, responsible AI, emerging technologies, and ethics. An avid life-long learner with an insatiable curiosity.\n\nᴄᴏʀᴇ ꜱᴋɪʟʟꜱ: Tech Leadership, Strategic Thinking, Emerging Tech, Applied Ethics. \n\n𝗧𝗛𝗘 𝗟𝗢𝗡𝗚𝗘𝗥 𝗦𝗧𝗢𝗥𝗬:\n\nI’m a seasoned tech leader with over two decades of experience leading and growing high-performance organisations, shaping cultures, and developing strategies to deliver technically complex, highly regulated programmes that meet organisational missions and deliver commercial and societal value at a predictable, sustainable pace. Embedded in my practices is a deep commitment to societal responsibility and trustworthiness.\n\nAs the co-founder and applied researcher at Urban Shed, I had the good fortune to launch several tech-for-good startups that combined emerging tech, applied ethics, and app development to showcase the value of people's awesome ideas within a sustainable, trustworthy, and responsible product development framework. My applied research continues with Jackdaw Labs and collaborations with organisations that are committed to a values-based digital future. Outside the tech space, I’m a Thai yoga massage therapist and mindfulness teacher, and practice observational-based photography inspired by nature.\n\nAlways open to a conversation about new projects and ideas, especially around machine learning, computer vision, AI, responsible innovation, ecology, security, ethics (Trust, Emerging Tech, and AI), mindfulness, neurodiversity, tech leadership and tech for good. I’m driven by a personal mission to make this world just that little bit better, and I've committed to raising the profile of women leaders and making neuro-inclusivity a given.\n\nEDUCATION HIGHLIGHTS\n\nKellogg College, University of Oxford: MSc Computer Science (Machine Learning)\n\nUniversity of Cambridge: Researched, developed and presented an applied ethical, pedagogical framework for Machine Learning/Artificial Intelligence practitioners\n\nCambridge Judge Business School: Cambridge General Management (GMCA)",
